WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en

00:00:00.000 --> 00:00:02.240
Good morning, Hank. It's Monday March 29th 2010.

00:00:02.250 --> 00:00:06.440
It's tax season, which means that I must once again for the 32nd consecutive year,

00:00:06.440 --> 00:00:10.640
report to the Internal Revenue Service that I have failed to garner any fishing boat proceeds.

00:00:10.640 --> 00:00:12.020
French the Llama!

00:00:12.020 --> 00:00:14.820
Fishing boat proceeds are the unicorns of my tax return.

00:00:14.820 --> 00:00:17.639
And now, Hank, having alienated all our new viewers,

00:00:17.640 --> 00:00:18.940
I begin the video.

00:00:18.940 --> 00:00:21.860
So Hank, I have a bad habit of googling myself

00:00:21.860 --> 00:00:24.080
--Hello Holden Caulfield Hat, how did you get here?--

00:00:24.080 --> 00:00:25.060
So the other day, I was googling myself,

00:00:25.060 --> 00:00:27.080
and I discovered something absolutely incredibly--

00:00:27.080 --> 00:00:28.000
Wait, wait, wait,

00:00:28.000 --> 00:00:29.200
I have something important to discuss,

00:00:29.200 --> 00:00:29.960
which is that in a few days,

00:00:29.960 --> 00:00:32.740
I have a book coming out that I co-wrote with David Levithan

00:00:32.740 --> 00:00:33.920
and we are going on tour

00:00:33.920 --> 00:00:36.140
and some of the events on the tour are ticketed, others are not,

00:00:36.140 --> 00:00:38.480
to find out if yours is ticketed, go to the link in the dooblydoo.

00:00:38.480 --> 00:00:40.180
Right Hank, so I discovered something pretty incredible,

00:00:40.180 --> 00:00:43.880
and it wasn't that there is a guy named John Green who is one of The Four Horsemen Of Sasquatchery.

00:00:43.880 --> 00:00:45.540
That I already knew

00:00:45.540 --> 00:00:47.660
because one time I wrote an article for Mental Floss Magazine

00:00:47.660 --> 00:00:50.520
in which I talked about the fact that Bigfoot is, you know, fictional

00:00:50.520 --> 00:00:52.980
and I totally got a letter from famous Bigfoot apologist,

00:00:52.980 --> 00:00:53.520
John Green,

00:00:53.520 --> 00:00:55.600
who said my anti-Bigfoot propaganda

00:00:55.600 --> 00:00:57.980
was besmirching the good name of John Greens everywhere.

00:00:57.980 --> 00:00:59.340
Right so that is not what I discovered.

00:00:59.340 --> 00:01:00.420
What I discovered

00:01:00.420 --> 00:01:01.480
--This is kind of annoying.

00:01:01.480 --> 00:01:02.000
The tassel!

00:01:02.010 --> 00:01:03.580
How did Holden deal with the tassel?--

00:01:03.580 --> 00:01:06.360
What I discovered is that a line I wrote years ago in my book,

00:01:06.360 --> 00:01:08.699
An Abundance Of Katherines, has kind of gone viral on Twitter.

00:01:08.700 --> 00:01:11.360
It's been tweeted more than 10,000 times.

00:01:11.360 --> 00:01:16.060
The line is "Che senso ha la vita se almeno non provi a fare qualcosa di significativo?".

00:01:16.060 --> 00:01:17.280
I don't speak Italian.

00:01:17.280 --> 00:01:18.280
The line is,

00:01:18.280 --> 00:01:19.620
"What is the point of being alive

00:01:19.620 --> 00:01:21.880
if you don't at least try to do something remarkable?

00:01:21.880 --> 00:01:23.960
Which I will admit I did write in this book,

00:01:23.960 --> 00:01:25.560
but as anyone who's read it knows,

00:01:25.560 --> 00:01:26.660
I was kidding.

00:01:26.660 --> 00:01:27.840
That's something Colin Singleton,

00:01:27.840 --> 00:01:29.680
the main character of the book - he's kind of a child prodigy,

00:01:29.680 --> 00:01:31.060
says at the beginning of the book

00:01:31.060 --> 00:01:33.460
and he must spend the entire book learning it's...

00:01:33.460 --> 00:01:34.140
bull.

00:01:34.140 --> 00:01:36.069
I don't know how it looks on the screen there on YouTube,

00:01:36.069 --> 00:01:37.140
but in the Viewfinder,

00:01:37.140 --> 00:01:39.260
It looks a little bit like a lady hat

00:01:39.260 --> 00:01:40.800
but I promise it is not,

00:01:40.800 --> 00:01:42.600
it is the actual replica

00:01:42.900 --> 00:01:44.220
the actual replica

00:01:44.220 --> 00:01:45.800
of a hat worn by

00:01:45.800 --> 00:01:48.220
a fictional character in a novel.

00:01:48.220 --> 00:01:49.320
Anyway, Hank,

00:01:49.320 --> 00:01:52.000
this phenomenon, it seems to me, is near universal in the internet age,

00:01:52.000 --> 00:01:54.040
which is that instead of people being misquoted,

00:01:54.040 --> 00:01:55.770
that they are miscontextualized.

00:01:55.770 --> 00:01:58.900
It's impossible to pull a line or a sentence or even a chapter from a book

00:01:58.900 --> 00:02:00.760
and understand the meaning of that section

00:02:00.760 --> 00:02:03.540
Because, as much as it pains us in this soundbite, twittering world,

00:02:03.540 --> 00:02:05.939
text means nothing without its context.

00:02:05.939 --> 00:02:08.460
For instance, Holden Caulfield's red hunting cap doesn't really make any sense

00:02:08.460 --> 00:02:12.320
until you have the context telling you that it's the same color as his dead brother's hair.

00:02:12.320 --> 00:02:14.120
Look, she's wearing a hat.

00:02:14.120 --> 00:02:14.960
It's not a metaphor.

00:02:14.960 --> 00:02:15.840
I just thought it was funny.

00:02:15.840 --> 00:02:17.420
Anyway, Hank, the whole thing made me think about something.

00:02:17.430 --> 00:02:19.460
Maybe our favorite quotations say more about us

00:02:19.460 --> 00:02:21.140
than they say about the stories and people we're quoting.

00:02:21.140 --> 00:02:21.960
So Nerdfighters,

00:02:21.960 --> 00:02:22.760
what's your favorite quote?

00:02:22.760 --> 00:02:23.960
Leave it down below in comments,

00:02:23.960 --> 00:02:25.960
and then let's think about what that says about us.

00:02:25.960 --> 00:02:28.140
I mean, how did Holden Caulfield deal with the tassel issue?

00:02:28.140 --> 00:02:29.500
Is that ever covered in the text?

00:02:29.500 --> 00:02:31.880
My favorite quote of the moment is from Robert Frost:

00:02:31.880 --> 00:02:32.820
"The only way out

00:02:32.820 --> 00:02:33.700
is through."

00:02:33.700 --> 00:02:35.820
I don't know what that says about me, but I'm sure you'll tell me in comments.

00:02:35.820 --> 00:02:37.960
And then we'll all tell each other what our quote says about each other.

00:02:37.960 --> 00:02:38.700
Okay,

00:02:38.700 --> 00:02:40.560
gotta get rid of this tassel, so annoying.

00:02:40.560 --> 00:02:42.600
Hank, I'll see you on Wednesday

00:02:42.600 --> 00:02:44.640
From some angles, it's a lady hat,

00:02:44.640 --> 00:02:45.820
and then from some angles, it's like,

00:02:45.820 --> 00:02:47.240
"It's-a me Mario!"

